  "style": {
    "id": "inkblot",
    "name": "Inkblot",
    "description": "Symmetric inkblots on heavy cream card in bright, soft daylight (original blots, not the Rorschach plates). Glossy beads of wet ink (black plus crimson and indigo) drip onto one half of an open sheet; the other half folds shut on a real hinge, a wooden press rubs it down, and the sheet peels open on a perfectly mirrored blot with noise-feathered bleeding edges, a pale capillary halo and darker pooled rims. The ink is a closed-form field (a sum of smooth elliptical blobs and voids from a seeded plan, warped by paper-fibre noise) evaluated per pixel in the card's shader in half-space, so both halves show the same function; wet ink is glossy and domed and dries to matte. Then the same blot is read several ways: pencil outlines (graphite on paper, chalk-white over ink) draw themselves on, suggest one thing, fade, and suggest another. The blot never changes, only the reading. Drip plips, the fold, the press, the peel and every pencil stroke are scored from the same plan.",
